Keep WebRTC's own echo canceller on Windows

The Windows audio device (AudioDeviceWindowsCore) makes Windows' Voice Capture DMO
(CLSID_CWMAudioAEC) and answered that a built-in echo canceller is available, so the
voice engine enabled the DMO and turned AEC3 off ("Disabling EC since built-in EC will be
used instead"; every ApplyConfig then has echo_canceller enabled 0). The DMO also records
the microphone itself, at 16 kHz in one channel (InitRecordingDMO), polled every 5 ms with
no delay given to the audio processing. None of the project's changes to AEC3 (patches
0009 to 0011, wrapper patch 0019) ran on Windows.

Measured in a loop of virtual devices (the Windows copy playing into VB-Cable's input and
recording from its output, unity gain; the other side a Linux copy whose microphone is a
speech-like signal): the DMO sent the other side's words back at full level, +10.5 to
+10.8 dB against their microphone, what any sound that is not cancelled gives in that
loop, where a Linux copy (AEC3) in its place sends nothing back (below the -63 dB floor).

With this patch, in the same loop on the same day (3 runs of the speech-like signal and 1
of a voice, 75 s each, against a run with the DMO between them that gave +10.7 dB again):
what goes back is at the floor of what the Windows copy sends with the loop cut, -62 to
-70 dB against the other side's microphone at the median in every window from the call's
first phrase on, the echo itself (the part linear in their words) at the estimator's own
floor, about -95 dB. Twice in the four runs the loop's delay grew later in the call (AEC3
moved its delay by 16 and 24 ms) and one phrase went back, at -28 and -56 dB, until AEC3
aligned again. With both talking at once (a voice played into the Windows copy's
microphone too) its own voice went out at +5.6 dB (+4.6 with the DMO) and the echo stayed
under the estimator's floor. The microphone is recorded at 48 kHz in the cable's two
channels and processed in one at 32 kHz: what the Windows copy sends now reaches 12 kHz,
the voice file's own limit (8 to 12 kHz: -21.7 dB against the whole, the file -21.3; the
DMO's stopped at 8 kHz, -64.6).

The device now answers that no built-in echo canceller is available. The voice engine
keeps AEC3, and the microphone is recorded through WASAPI as without the DMO: the device's
shared-mode format (48 kHz first, patch 0004's rates included), on the capture thread
registered with MMCSS as before, with the recording delay WASAPI reports. The DMO is
still made at construction and never enabled. Endpoint notifications (patches 0003 and
0011) do not depend on the capture path.

A device that takes none of the formats InitRecording tries (48, 44.1, 16, 96, 32 and 8
kHz, then 176.4 and 192, each in two, one or four channels, asked one by one with
IsFormatSupported in shared mode) had no microphone at all on that path, where the DMO
converted any device. It is now recorded at 48 kHz (in one channel if its mix format has
one, else in two) with Windows' audio engine converting from its mix format
(AUDCLNT_STREAMFLAGS_AUTOCONVERTPCM with SRC_DEFAULT_QUALITY on Initialize, which then
takes a PCM format that is not the mix format; IsFormatSupported is not asked for it), and
WebRTC's log says so with the device's mix format. A device that takes one of the formats
tried records exactly as before. InitRecordingWithConversionForTesting() takes that path
for any device: wrapper patch 0024's test records the first capture device so.
InitPlayout has the same search and no such fallback, as before this patch: a render
device that takes none of its formats still fails to start playout.

The fallback's format names its speakers (KSAUDIO_SPEAKER_MONO or _STEREO) for Windows'
channel matrixer, which mixes a device with more channels down to them; the exact formats
keep the mask 0 they had.

The plain capture thread differs from the DMO's in two more ways, made as the DMO's:
- It read the keyboard: KeyPressed() polls every key with GetAsyncKeyState every 10 ms
  for a typing flag, a pattern security tools take for a key logger. The flag is now
  false; in this branch the audio processing only writes it into its debug recordings
  (AudioProcessingImpl's key_pressed reaches the AEC dump alone: no typing detection or
  transient suppression uses it).
- It ended the recording for good after 0.5 s without a capture event. It now waits on,
  saying so in the log (at the first half second and every 10 s), and still ends at
  once on the shutdown event; a stream that no longer works after a stall fails in
  GetBuffer. The DMO's thread polled every 5 ms and never ended on a stall.
A stream that fails (AUDCLNT_E_DEVICE_INVALIDATED: the device unplugged, its format
changed in the Sound settings, the audio service restarted) still ends the capture
thread, as a failing ProcessOutput ended the DMO's: the microphone stays silent (the
application says it cannot use it after 8 s) until it is turned off and on again or
another input is chosen; an input chosen by name that comes back elsewhere in the list is
chosen again by the plugin (KeepRecordingDevice), the system's default is not. The same
as with the DMO.

Windows' capture effects (the driver's audio processing for the stream's category) apply
to this stream as to any other default-category stream; the DMO opened the device in its
own way. How a device's effects differ between the two is not known here: a laptop's
built-in microphone, for one, may sound different. That is for the test on real
hardware. And AEC3 removes only what this application plays: other applications' sound
on the speakers is no longer taken out of the microphone (the DMO used the whole output
mix as its reference), as on Linux and macOS.

Known: in the PC-Windows call check the VM plays a steady tone on the cable that is also
the Windows copy's microphone (the shared screen's sound). With a pure tone as the Windows
copy's microphone the far side's echo canceller (AEC3 on Linux too) holds its own voice
down at times, at a call's start or after an unmute: with a tone of another frequency
than the far side's, 12 of 17 calls passed ("too little audio from Ana" the failure;
unmutes heard up to 20 s late); with the same 440 Hz tone on both sides, 1 of 3. With a
recorded voice in the VM instead, 31 of 31 calls passed, every unmute heard within 1 s.
The published library passed all 16 calls with a tone. No real Windows hardware was
tried.

